Task: 5
The Nautilus system admins team has rolled out a web UI application for their backup utility on the Nautilus backup server within the Stratos Datacenter. This application operates on port 5000, and firewalld is active on the server. To meet operational needs, the following requirements have been identified:
Allow all incoming connections on port 5000/tcp. Ensure the zone is set to public.
Solution:
✅ Steps to Allow Port 5000 in firewalld
1.	Check active zone (it should be public as per requirement):
2.	sudo firewall-cmd --get-active-zones
3.	Add rule for port 5000/tcp in the public zone:
4.	sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-port=5000/tcp --permanent
o	--zone=public → ensures rule is applied to the public zone.
o	--add-port=5000/tcp → allows incoming traffic on port 5000 (TCP).
o	--permanent → makes the change persistent across reboots.
5.	Reload firewall to apply changes:
6.	sudo firewall-cmd --reload
7.	Verify the rule:
8.	sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-ports
You should see:
5000/tcp
________________________________________
👉 Now the backup web UI app running on port 5000 will be accessible, and the firewall rules are aligned with security best practices.





