Task: 62
To secure our Nautilus infrastructure in Stratos Datacenter we have decided to install and configure firewalld on all app servers. We have Apache and Nginx services running on these apps. Nginx is running as a reverse proxy server for Apache. We might have more robust firewall settings in the future, but for now we have decided to go with the given requirements listed below:

a. Allow all incoming connections on Nginx port, i.e 80.
b. Block all incoming connections on Apache port, i.e 8080.
c. All rules must be permanent.
d. Zone should be public.
e. If Apache or Nginx services aren't running already, please make sure to start them.

Solution: 

1. Install firewalld
sudo yum install firewalld -y     # RHEL/CentOS
# or
sudo apt-get install firewalld -y # Ubuntu/Debian
2. Enable and start firewalld
sudo systemctl enable firewalld
sudo systemctl start firewalld
3. Allow Nginx (port 80) in the public zone
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-port=80/tcp --permanent
4. Block Apache (port 8080) in the public zone
First remove if it’s already allowed:
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --remove-port=8080/tcp --permanent
(Blocking is the default deny behavior in firewalld, so you just need to make sure it’s not allowed.)
5. Reload firewall to apply changes
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
6. Verify rules
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-all
You should see ports: 80/tcp but not 8080/tcp.
7. Ensure Apache and Nginx services are running
# For Apache (port 8080)
sudo systemctl enable httpd
sudo systemctl start httpd
# For Nginx (port 80)
sudo systemctl enable nginx
sudo systemctl start nginx
________________________________________
✅ Final state:
•	Port 80 (Nginx) → Allowed
•	Port 8080 (Apache) → Blocked
•	Both services → Running
•	Rules → Permanent in public zone
