Task: 86
The Nautilus DevOps team is ready to launch a new application, which they will deploy on app servers in Stratos Datacenter. They are expecting significant traffic/usage of squid on app servers after that. This will generate massive logs, creating huge log files. To utilise the storage efficiently, they need to compress the log files and need to rotate old logs. Check the requirements shared below:
a. In all app servers install squid package.
b. Using logrotate configure squid logs rotation to monthly and keep only 3 rotated logs.
(If by default log rotation is set, then please update configuration as needed)
Solution:
Step 1: Install Squid
1.	Connect to each app server.
2.	Install Squid using yum (for CentOS/RHEL-based systems):
sudo yum install squid -y
3.	Start and enable the Squid service:
sudo systemctl start squid
sudo systemctl enable squid
4.	Verify that Squid is running:
systemctl status squid
Step 2: Configure Log Rotation with Logrotate
1.	Open or create a logrotate configuration file for Squid. Usually, it’s at /etc/logrotate.d/squid.
sudo vi /etc/logrotate.d/squid
2.	Add the following configuration (or update if it exists):
/var/log/squid/*.log {
    monthly
    rotate 3
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
    sharedscripts
    postrotate
        systemctl reload squid > /dev/null 2>/dev/null || true
    endscript}
Explanation:
•	monthly → Rotate logs every month.
•	rotate 3 → Keep only the last 3 rotated logs.
•	compress → Compress old logs to save space.
•	missingok → Don’t error if logs are missing.
•	notifempty → Don’t rotate empty logs.
•	postrotate → Reload Squid to start a new log file after rotation.
Step 3: Test Logrotate
Run logrotate manually to ensure configuration works:
sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/squid   # Dry run (no changes)
sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.d/squid   # Force rotation
✅ After this, Squid logs will rotate monthly, compress old logs, and retain only 3 rotated files.

